To: VP, Supply Chain and Operations Leadership
From: Sustainability & Logistics Director
Subject: Network shipping plan balancing cost and carbon impact
Date: 

Context
Our distribution network must reliably move product from multiple warehouses to multiple stores while managing both financial performance and our carbon footprint. Each warehouse-to-store lane is evaluated on both monetary cost per unit and carbon intensity per unit. In many cases, the lowest-cost option carries a higher emissions burden. We need a clear, repeatable way to balance these competing goals across the network.

Operational rules to honor
- Route activation fees: Starting shipments on any lane requires an upfront setup cost tied to contracting, systems, and dock preparation. A lane is either active or not, and once active the fee applies regardless of actual volume shipped.
- Minimum order quantities: If a lane is activated, we will move at least a specified minimum volume on that lane to justify handling and transport commitments. We will not open lanes for partial loads below that threshold.
- Supplier limits at the store: To keep receiving manageable and aligned with dock capacity and vendor oversight, each store may be served by only a capped number of warehouses at any time.

Supply and demand realities
- Warehouse shipments are bounded by available inventory and handling capacity at each origin.
- Each store requires at least its planned inbound volume to protect availability and service levels.

Management priority
Leadership has established importance weights that reflect how we value cost versus emissions. We will evaluate candidate shipping plans using a blended score that multiplies variable shipping costs by the cost weight, adds emissions multiplied by the emissions weight, and includes all route setup fees at their full amounts (setup fees are not weighted). This approach allows us to trade off dollars and carbon in a transparent way consistent with current priorities.

Goal
Identify which warehouse-to-store lanes to activate and the volume to move on each active lane such that:
- Warehouse inventory limits are respected,
- Every store’s demand requirement is met,
- Any activated lane ships at least its minimum order quantity,
- Each store is supplied by no more than the allowed number of warehouses,
- The resulting blended score (cost- and emissions-weighted variable spend plus full setup fees) is as low as possible under these priorities.

Deliverable
A route activation list and shipment plan that meets the above rules, a summary of expected transportation spend and emissions, and a clear view of trade-offs (e.g., cost savings versus incremental emissions) for leadership approval. Sensitivity scenarios will show how recommendations shift if weights or minimums change.

ANNEX – Data Template
- Number of source warehouses: {NUM_SOURCES}
- Number of destination stores: {NUM_DESTINATIONS}
- Available supply at each warehouse (units): {SUPPLIES}
- Minimum demand at each store (units): {DEMANDS}
- Variable shipping cost per unit by lane (origin × destination): {COST_MATRIX}
- Emission rate per unit by lane (kg CO2): {EMISSION_MATRIX}
- Cost importance weight: {WEIGHT_COST}
- Emission importance weight: {WEIGHT_EMISSION}
- Upfront setup cost to activate each lane: {FIXED_COSTS}
- Minimum order quantity for any activated lane (units): {MIN_ORDER_QUANTITIES}
- Maximum number of suppliers allowed per store: {MAX_SUPPLIERS_PER_STORE}
